  "query_preview": "..A weakening upper low centered near the far SoCal \ncoast continues to spread moisture north through Arizona ahead of \nit. This moisture is enhanced by remnants of former Tropical Storm\nSimon. Precipitation associated with the moisture is noted\nover/near the Utah/Arizona border this evening.\n\nAs the storm continues to fall apart Sunday and Monday, weak vort\nlobes are progged to eject into Utah. Otherwise, the bulk of the \nremnant energy will move across Arizona. With abundant moisture \nbut weak dynamics, expect mainly spotty showers or disorganized\nbands of precipitation through Monday associated with this storm,\nmainly over southern and central Utah.\n\nAttention then shifts to the next storm system, which will \ndevelop into a closed low off the central Cal coast by Tuesday. \nWith diffluent flow aloft, expect precipitation to increase on \nTuesday ahead of this storm. The upper jet aiding the upper \ndiffluence will move out by Wednesday, and with the upper low \nstill positioned off the coast, Wednesday could see a relative \nlull in precipitation. The low is then progged to move inland on \nThursday and across Utah on Friday, bringing a period of more \nwidespread precipitation to the area. Some moisture from remnants \nof what is currently Hurricane Trudy near the west coast of Mexico\nmay catch portions of Utah during this time. Additionally, as \ncooler air arrives late Thursday into Friday snow levels will \nlower, potentially falling to as low as 8kt. However, subtle \ndifferences in the track and evolution of this low will have an \neffect on the eventual snow levels, which could remain above 9kft \nbased on some solutions.\n\nOverall, the NBM has a general 1 to 2+ inches of QPF in the\nmountains from Tuesday through Friday, with around half of that \nin most valleys. The snow amount forecast will be trickier due to\naforementioned snow level uncertainties, but the current NBM has\n2-7 inches along the higher ridgelines (generally above 9000 ft).\n\nBeyond Friday, greater models spread exists. While many solutions\nhave the storm system out of the area for Saturday, around 55% of\nglobal ensemble members have some remnant troughiness hanging back\nover Utah (something which has been especially favored by the \nlatest runs of the GFS and its ensemble members). This would\nmaintain cooler and unsettled weather over the area into next\nweekend, compared to the drier solution offered by the EC and a \nminority of global ensemble members.",
